#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100638 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e092e191-84ac-5d9f-b0db-94d628a6154e" :question/math-academy-id "e-525" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$y' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0$\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n2. Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n3. The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For $x\\ne0$, differentiate $f(x)=x^{-1}$:\n$$f'(x)=-x^{-2}=-\\frac1{x^2}.$$\nThus statement I is true. Substitution into the right-hand side gives\n$$-\\frac{f(x)}x=-\\frac{1/x}x=-\\frac1{x^2}=f'(x),$$\nso II is true. This identity holds throughout either interval $x>0$ or $x<0$, making $f$ a solution and III true. The answer is **I, II, and III**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82a9b23c-4c79-56cd-9761-79d0ee22646d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Initial Value Problems"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5ffa549-e77e-5db4-9fde-5b0134957539" :question/math-academy-id "e-8732" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2$.\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2$.\n\nSubstituting the initial condition\n\n$$\ny({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}\n$$\n\ninto our solution and solving for $A$, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}y & = A {x}^{3} \\\\ \\textcolor{blue}{- 2} & = A ( \\textcolor{red}{- 1} {)}^{3} \\\\ - 2 & = A ( - 1 ) \\\\ A & = 2.\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n$y = 2x^3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100714 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1228900d-87d4-5ff0-8e01-ff9417e82220" :question/math-academy-id "e-9978" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y$.\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $y = e^x + 1$\n2. $y = -e^x$\n3. $y = 2e^x$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "A solution must satisfy $y'=y$ at every point of its interval. Differentiate each candidate and compare.\n\n- I: If $y=e^x+1$, then $y'=e^x\\ne e^x+1=y$. Therefore $y=e^x+1$ is not a solution.\n- II: If $y=-e^x$, then $y'=-e^x=y$. It is a solution.\n- III: If $y=2e^x$, then $y'=2e^x=y$. It is a solution.\n\nThe correct answer is **II and III only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b393b878-c5bd-525b-95d3-a6f539ad09f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098191} {:answer/id #uuid "cc57abfc-d7d4-5bbe-b820-47d6bcfddc83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098192} {:answer/id #uuid "f102d687-4edb-5d57-a6d8-6e06a4e9175f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098193} {:answer/id #uuid "01ed849a-83c5-5a2e-9479-ecd16db6de49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098194} {:answer/id #uuid "40cebea2-5d0d-5d53-9583-e01b08ec8cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40cebea2-5d0d-5d53-9583-e01b08ec8cff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098195} :answer-field/id #uuid "23c7473c-7e8a-5122-8097-f7924bdb001a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47b66471-b70f-5509-8368-6a6674b74447" :question/math-academy-id "q-141078" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$y^{\\prime } = 2y$\n\nLet $f(x) = e^{2x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f^{\\prime }(x) = 2e^{2x}$\n2. Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y^{\\prime } = f^{\\prime }(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n3. The function $y = f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The chain rule gives $f'(x)=2e^{2x}$, so I is true. The right-hand side is $2f(x)=2e^{2x}=f'(x)$ for all $x$, so II is true and $f$ is a solution, making III true. Select **I, II, and III**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b5098d2-8759-5c88-873b-59c47317c676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100303} {:answer/id #uuid "bef0901a-3e08-5d64-8008-69c96233a746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100304} {:answer/id #uuid "ef60a1c2-3509-51f2-bca5-8747b265f89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100305} {:answer/id #uuid "1164be35-3601-5d59-bd65-4712ef249554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100306} {:answer/id #uuid "14f11516-8f03-5153-b333-a0fbd914b758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1164be35-3601-5d59-bd65-4712ef249554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100306} :answer-field/id #uuid "917fb1fb-b6b6-5fde-815a-f160d6a1eef4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a773fc2b-812d-5f5c-8cbb-9b1672c48928" :question/math-academy-id "q-79335" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2y}{x} - 2, x \\ne 0$.\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $y = 2x$\n2. $y = x^{2}$\n3. $y = x^{3}$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For $x\\ne0$, compare each derivative with $2y/x-2$.\n\n- I: for $y=2x$, $y'=2$ and $2y/x-2=4-2=2$, so it works.\n- II: for $y=x^2$, $y'=2x$ but $2y/x-2=2x-2$, so it fails.\n- III: for $y=x^3$, $y'=3x^2$ but $2y/x-2=2x^2-2$, so it fails.\n\nSelect **I only**."}]}
